  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the two solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x+\\sqrt{3}=2\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}} and $x=$ \n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x+\\sqrt{3} & =2\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}\u2061x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=\\frac{3}{-\\sqrt{3}}=-\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arctan}(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\pi +x=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2\\pi +x=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$",
